#{[17592186125780 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/7e/7ef5d72a30dd7f79f6182c4d78d705a7771f9f90ff0bb50f6d539614cb1e5b4d.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125780 #edb/ref 1065 3522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125780 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "555ca00c-d37e-5c67-a658-69b087390920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125780 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125807 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's state the previous result more generally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a function that's continuously differentiable at the point $P(x_0,y_0).$   If  $\\nabla f(x_0, y_0)$ is not the zero vector, then it is orthogonal to the tangent line to the level curve \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nat the point $P.$ The equation of the tangent can then be written as\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\! f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nWriting this explicitly, we get\n\n$$\nf_x(x_0,y_0)(x-x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y-y_0)   = 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125807 #edb/ref 1065 3866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125807 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9c2ee0b-08cf-5f42-bf09-f80dcd82051d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125807 #edb/ref 1067 "Tangent Line to a Level Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now look at an example of a subset of a vector space that is not a subspace.\n\nConsider the vector space $\\mathbb R_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ at+b \\: | \\: a,b  \\in\\mathbb{Z} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition. Indeed, suppose that $f(t)=a_1t+b_1$ and $g(t)=a_2t+b_2$ are two polynomials from $B.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})t+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $B$ since $a_1+a_2$ and $b_1+b_2$ are in $\\Bbb Z.$\n\n- $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication. As a counterexample, consider $f(t)=t$ and $k=0.5,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =0.5t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial from $B,$ because $0.5 \\notin \\Bbb Z.$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is not a subspace of $\\mathbb R_2[t].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1065 6193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "532d5411-c233-58a0-88e3-bd13bc8fcd1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1067 "Example of a Subset That Is Not a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133877 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175921863